+## Release-tag bypass (mint-released repos)
+
+A repo that releases via [`mint`](https://github.com/bounded-systems/mint) pushes a
+`v*` tag, and its `release.yml` / `publish-jsr.yml` fire on that
+push. An **active ruleset that restricts ref creation on `refs/tags/*`** with no
+bypass for the release identity breaks that silently: `git push origin v1.2.3`
+dies with `GH013 creations restricted` (or the Git-DB API returns 422 with no
+ruleset named), JSR still publishes via CI dispatch, and the GitHub Release,
+binaries and provenance never appear. Enterprise-level rulesets are the usual
+culprit — they are not visible in the repo/org ruleset UI.
+[`scripts/audit/release-tags.ts`](scripts/audit/release-tags.ts) surfaces that as a
+`repo-config` finding at the gate instead
+([conformance#25](https://github.com/bounded-systems/conformance/issues/25),
+implementing [trellis#34](https://github.com/bounded-systems/trellis/issues/34)):
+
+| column | how |
+|---|---|
+| mint-released | a `.github/workflows/*.yml` on `push: tags: [v*]`, a `.release/` intents dir, or a call to mint's `release-cut.yml` |
+| tag creation restricted | `GET repos/{o}/{r}/rulesets?targets=tag&includes_parents=true` → any **active** ruleset with a `creation` rule whose `ref_name` condition covers `refs/tags/v*` |
+| release-identity bypass | a `bypass_actors` entry with `bypass_mode: always` for the release identity |
+| verdict | `SKIP` (not mint-released) · `PASS` · `FAIL` · `UNKNOWN` (bypass actors not readable with this token) |
+
+```sh
+deno task audit:release-tags                                  # per-repo table + summary, exit 0
+deno task audit:release-tags -- --release-actor=github-actions  # require the Actions app (GITHUB_TOKEN, mint's release-cut path)
+deno task audit:release-tags -- --repo=mint --strict            # one repo; exit 1 on FAIL
+deno task test:audit                                            # unit tests against fixture rulesets (no network in the tests themselves)
+```
+
+trellis#34 has not yet settled *where* a repo declares its release identity, so
+the check takes it as `--release-actor` (`any` | `github-actions` | `org-admin` |
+`integration:<id>` | `team:<id>` | `role:<id>` | `deploy-key:<id>`). Until one is
+declared, any `always` bypass actor counts and the actor list is printed as
+evidence for a reviewer to judge.
+
+**Token scope.** Repo enumeration, trees, contents and the rulesets *list* work
+with the default token. `bypass_actors` is only returned to a token with **write
+access to the ruleset**: an org-admin token (`admin:org` — the same App
+`scripts/audit.mjs` and `open-prs.yml` use) for org rulesets, and enterprise
+ownership for an enterprise-level one. With a lesser token every restricted repo
+scores `UNKNOWN`, never a false `PASS`. `release-tags-audit.yml` mints the org-admin
+App token when `ORG_ADMIN_APP_ID` / `ORG_ADMIN_APP_PRIVATE_KEY` are set.
+
